From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Migrate 0414-net-03-barrier-before-read.patch

Source: 0414-net-03-barrier-before-read.patch
shader: EDEN_NX2_NET_BARRIER, keep a warp's shared memory accesses in order

Applies after 0413 of test/T28-upscaler-safe. Off unless EDEN_NX2_NET_BARRIER=1.

A warp's shared memory accesses stay in order on the console, so native programs may store rows
and read other lanes' rows back with no BAR between them. Ocarina of Time's upscaler does this
after its last BAR (agl_ndsr_vr_light.pck, program at 0x1be00: BAR 0x4c70, nine STS 0x4c90 to
0x4d20, LDS from 0x4d40). A host compiler may move those accesses past each other. On Metal it
did: about 100 pixels a frame came out wrong, up to 1.07, in all three channels at fixed tile
positions, and two identical dispatches differed.

EDEN_NX2_NET_BARRIER=1: in a program with HMMA, the first shared load after a store and before
the next BAR gets a subgroup memory barrier. A new IR op, SubgroupMemoryBarrier, because
MEMBAR.CTA here means workgroup scope with uniform and image memory as well, which waits for
more than this needs. SPIR-V: OpMemoryBarrier, Subgroup scope, AcquireRelease | WorkgroupMemory.
The lowering marks it with bit 20 of a Maxwell MEMBAR, a field Maxwell does not use.

Ocarina's upscaler gets two barriers, 24 bytes of SPIR-V. On Metal the race went from 1,228
differing pixels over 12 runs to none in 24 runs on two frames, with no measurable cost (27.5 ms
against 27.4 ms at 1080p).

Off: the module is byte for byte the same as without this patch, for this program. Not run on
the console or on RADV. The guest warp must sit inside one host subgroup; subgroup sizes 32 and
64 both satisfy that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

